1:["$","$Ld",null,{"item":{"id":"define-at-use","number":"03","title":"Put the meaning next to the symbol.","mode":"Notation overload","scale":"slide","dek":"Define the symbols a listener needs on the slide where they are used. Explain what the expression says in economic terms.","before":[{"id":"notation","kind":"text","title":"Notation","provenance":"Schematic reconstruction","sourcePage":"5, 8–13, 17","lines":["\\(c\\): local labor market · \\(i\\): industry","\\(M\\): robot stock · \\(L\\): employment · \\(Y\\): industry output","\\(0\\) and \\(t\\): beginning and end of the period · \\(g^Y\\): output growth","\\(\\ell_{ci}\\): industry \\(i\\)’s local employment share in 1970","\\(\\overline{\\mathrm{APR}}_i\\): adjusted robot penetration in Europe","\\(E_c\\): local robot exposure, weighted by those employment shares"]},{"title":"Exposure equation · PDF p. 17","kind":"image","image":"/originals/robots-jobs-p17.png","alt":"Three exposure formulas with time subscripts and no adjacent key for M, L, or output growth.","label":"LOCAL ROBOT EXPOSURE","id":"exposure","sourcePage":"17"}],"after":[{"title":"Past industry shares predict local robot exposure.","kind":"equation","formula":"E_c=\\sum_i \\ell_{ci}\\,\\overline{\\mathrm{APR}}_i","revises":["exposure","notation"],"terms":[{"symbol":"E_c","meaning":"Predicted exposure in local labor market \\(c\\)","fullWidth":true},{"symbol":"\\ell_{ci}","meaning":"Industry \\(i\\)’s share of local employment in 1970"},{"symbol":"\\overline{\\mathrm{APR}}_i","meaning":"Adjusted robot penetration in European industry \\(i\\)"}]},{"title":"“Adjusted” removes an output-growth component.","kind":"equation","formula":"\\mathrm{APR}=\\frac{M_t-M_0}{L_0}-g^Y\\frac{M_0}{L_0}","revises":["exposure","notation"],"terms":[{"symbol":"M_0,\\ M_t","meaning":"Robot stock at the start and end of the period","fullWidth":true},{"symbol":"L_0","meaning":"Employment at the start of the period"},{"symbol":"g^Y","meaning":"Industry output growth over the period"}],"subtitle":"Adjusted robot penetration"}],"diagnosis":"The page already explains European adoption and the 1970 weights. Its formulas still ask the audience to retrieve several symbols from elsewhere.","changes":["Teach the weighted sum in words.","Attach a local key to the same expression.","Explain the adjustment on a second build."],"preserved":"The weighted exposure construction, 1970 baseline, European instrument, and output-growth adjustment.","deferred":"The US exposure formula stays in the source. Suppressing indices is disclosed; validity is not inferred from the instrument’s construction.","practice":"Cover every slide except the one on screen. Can a listener recover what each load-bearing symbol means?","principle":"Takeaways before details","sourceType":"public","context":"The source defines the European comparison and 1970 weights. The revision adds a local notation key.","beforeCaption":"","afterCaption":"","source":{"authors":"Daron Acemoglu, Pascual Restrepo","title":"Robots and Jobs","date":"2019-04","url":"https://www.yjs.fi/wp-content/uploads/daron-acemoglu_robots-and-jobs_part-1.pdf","pages":"5, 8–13, 17"},"topic":"Labor & technology","point":"Listeners may forget a definition or miss the moment when it is introduced. Put short definitions beside the expressions that depend on them, even if the notation appeared earlier. State what the calculation measures or what the equation says about economic behavior, so the audience can recover the argument while looking at the current slide."}}]
